10:12:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Software and game updates usually do **not get sent directly to every device all at once**. Instead, the developer publishes the new version to an update distribution system, and devices download it when they check for updates. Typical process: 1. **Developer builds the update** - New code, files, bug fixes, etc. - The update is tested and digitally signed so devices can verify it is genuine. 2. **It is uploaded to a platform or server** - Mobile apps: Apple App Store, Google Play Store. - PC games: Steam, Epic Games Store, Xbox app, Battle.net, etc. - Console games: PlayStation Network, Xbox Network, Nintendo eShop. - Operating systems: Windows Update, macOS Software Update, Android/iOS update servers. - Some software uses its own updater and servers. 3. **The update is copied to many download servers** - Large companies use a **CDN** (Content Delivery Network): servers located around the world. - A person in Japan may download from a nearby Asian server, while someone in Europe downloads from a European one. This avoids one central server being overloaded. 4. **Devices periodically check whether an update exists** - The app store, game launcher, operating system, or app itself asks something like: “Is there a newer version available for this device?” - This may happen automatically in the background, when the device is charging/on Wi‑Fi, or when the user opens the app/launcher. 5. **The device downloads and installs it** - If automatic updates are enabled, it may happen without the user noticing. - Otherwise, the user sees an Update button or a notification. - Often only changed files are downloaded—called a **delta/patch update**—rather than downloading the entire game again. 6. **Updates are often released gradually** - Companies may first give an update to 1% of users, then 10%, then everyone. - This is called a **staged rollout** or **phased release**. - It helps catch serious bugs before they affect every player/device. For online games, there are usually two separate things: - **Game client update:** downloaded by each player’s console/PC/phone. - **Server update:** installed by the game company on its own servers. Players do not download this part; they simply connect to the updated server. So, the short version is: the company publishes one update to app stores or download servers, and each device independently discovers and downloads it, often through nearby CDN servers.
