10:12:22 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=628
10:12:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It can definitely *look* like it goes to everyone at once, especially for online games. What usually happens is: - The developer schedules the update to become available at a specific time. - At that time, the game launcher, console, phone, or app store sees that a new version is required. - Everyone who opens the game—or whose device checks for updates around then—starts downloading the same patch from nearby download servers. For many multiplayer games, the developer also updates the **game servers** at the same scheduled time. The servers may reject older versions of the game, so all players are effectively forced to update before playing. That is why you and your friends may all get the update at nearly the same moment. But the company generally is not individually transmitting a separate copy from one server directly to every player. Instead: - One release is published. - It is replicated across many CDN/download servers worldwide. - Millions of devices request and download it simultaneously. - Each device may be served by a different nearby server. Sometimes it really is effectively a worldwide “everyone update now” release—especially for a major game patch, season launch, or security-required update. Other times, updates are rolled out gradually, but that is more common for ordinary mobile apps and operating systems than for multiplayer game patches.
