10:12:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You *can* say an update is “distributed to every device” in a general, everyday sense—especially when it becomes available to everyone at the same scheduled time. But technically, it is different from a radio broadcast: - **Radio:** one transmitted signal is received by every radio within range at the same time. The station sends the audio once, and any number of radios can listen without creating extra work for the station. - **Game update over the internet:** each device normally downloads its own copy of the update. If 1 million players update, the network delivers roughly 1 million copies’ worth of data—usually from many CDN servers, not one central server. So a game company may announce or enable the update for everyone at once, much like a broadcast schedule. But the actual game files are usually delivered through many individual internet downloads, not one shared signal that every device “hears.” There are technologies closer to broadcasting—such as multicast, satellite distribution, or local-network broadcasting—but they are not how most consumer game updates are delivered over the public internet.
